 "fieldData": {
  "awb---faq-data": "<div data-rt-embed-type='true'><div data-rt-embed-type='true'><script> window.awbFAQ = {\"heading\": \"Home Care Intake Questions, Answered\", \"items\": [{\"q\": \"What is a home care intake form?\", \"a\": \"A home care intake form collects what a non-medical home care agency needs before care starts: the client's details, family contacts, help needed with daily activities, home hazards, and the hours wanted. Agencies can fill part of it on the first call and the rest at an in-home assessment.\"}, {\"q\": \"How can I create my own home care intake form?\", \"a\": \"Write down the questions your coordinator and care managers ask today, then describe them to Emergent's <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-form-builder'>AI form builder</a>. It builds the form and a table that holds every client record. Ask for changes in plain English whenever your process changes.\"}, {\"q\": \"What is a good example of a home care intake form?\", \"a\": \"A typical one has two parts. A relative fills in the inquiry part: the client's address, family contacts in calling order, and how care will be paid for. The care manager completes the in-home part, and Emergent can build both parts on one client record. A general <a href='https://emergent.sh/ai-form-builder/intake-form'>intake form</a> for a law firm or coach follows the same pattern with different questions.\"}, {\"q\": \"What other forms go with a home care intake form?\", \"a\": \"A service agreement with rates and visit hours, the signed care plan from the assessment, and an emergency contact form the caregiver can open on shift. Kept as sections of one client record, each starts with the client's name and address already filled in.\"}, {\"q\": \"When should a client fill out a home care intake form?\", \"a\": \"Before the first service. In home care, a relative can fill in the inquiry section right after the first call, while the details are fresh. The care manager completes the rest with the client at the in-home assessment.\"}, {\"q\": \"Does a non-medical home care intake form need medical questions?\", \"a\": \"A few, kept short. Non-medical care means help with daily tasks, not treatment. A caregiver on shift still needs to know about allergies and mobility aids, and how to reach the client's doctor. Leave diagnoses and clinical history out unless the agency also provides skilled home health services.\"}, {\"q\": \"How does a home care assessment differ from a home care intake form?\", \"a\": \"The assessment is one stage of intake. Intake opens with the phone inquiry, which collects family contacts and the payer. The assessment comes later, at the house, where the care manager scores daily activities to set the starting hours. Built with Emergent, both stages write to the same client table instead of two separate files.\"}, {\"q\": \"What should an intake form for home health care include?\", \"a\": \"Start with the clinical questions a non-medical form leaves out, such as diagnoses and current medications. Add the ordering doctor and the nursing or therapy visits on the order. Family contacts and the house safety check carry over unchanged.\"}, {\"q\": \"Can I make a free home care intake form?\", \"a\": \"Yes. Emergent is free to start, and the free tier covers building and publishing a form with a live URL. Responses have no cap. Exporting the code to GitHub comes with the paid plans.\"}, {\"q\": \"Is a home health intake form the same as a home care one?\", \"a\": \"No. Home health means skilled services such as part-time nursing or therapy, and a home health intake form asks clinical questions to match. Home care is personal help with tasks like bathing or dressing. On the clinical side, home health intake sits closer to a patient intake form.\"}]}; </script></div></div>"
 }
}